'''apocryphal''' (a-pok′ri-fal), ''a.'' and ''n.'' [< NL. ''apocryphalis'', < LL. ''apocrypha'': see ''apocrypha.''] '''I.''' ''a.'' '''1.''' Of doubtful authorship, authenticity, or inspiration; spurious; fictitious; false.
{{smaller block|The ''apocryphal'' relics of saints and apostles which then burdened the shrines of Greek churches.
{{right|''Ticknor'', Span. Lit., I. 185.}}}}
Specifically—'''2.''' ''Eccles.'': (''a'') Of doubtful sanction; uncanonical; having no ecclesiastical authority.
{{smaller block|Jerome . . . saith that all writings not canonical are ''apocryphal.''
{{right|''Hooker.''}}}}
(''b'') Of or pertaining to the Apocrypha: as, “the ''Apocryphal'' writers,” ''Addison.''

'''II.''' ''n.'' A writing not canonical; a book or passage of uncertain source, authority, or credit. [Rare.]
{{smaller block|Nicephorus and Anastasius, . . . because they were interpolated and corrupted, did rank these epistles in the number of ''apocryphals.''
{{right|''Hanmer'', Eccles. Antiq., p. 419.}}}}

'''apocryphalist''' (a̤-pok′ri-fa̤l-ist), ''n.'' [< ''apocryphal'' + ''-ist.''] An advocate of the canonicity of the Apocrypha.

'''apocryphally''' (a̤-pok′ri-fa̤l-i), ''adv.'' In an apocryphal manner; uncertainly; equivocally; doubtfully.

