'''Apocynum''' (a-pos′i-num), ''n.'' [NL., < L. ''apocynon'', dogbane (''Aconitum lycoctonum'', Linnæus), < Gr. ἀπόκυνον, a plant, ''Cynanchus erectus'', < ἀπό, from, away, + κύων (κυν-), a dog, = E. ''hound''.] Dogbane, a genus of perennial herbs, type of the natural order ''Apocynaceæ'' (which see), and including three species, of which two, ''A. androsæmifolium'' and ''A. cannabinum'', are North American. {{smaller|The common name of the latter is ''Indian hemp'', from the use of its fibrous and extremely tough bark by the American Indians for making nets, etc.}}

'''apod, apode''' (ap′od, -ōd), ''a.'' and ''n.'' [< NL. ''apus'' (''apod-''), < Gr. ἄπους (ἀποδ-), footless, < ἀ- priv. + πούς (ποδ-) = E. ''foot''.] '''I.''' ''a.'' Footless; apodal.

'''II.''' ''n.'' An apodal or apodous animal; an animal without feet, or supposed to have none; a member of one of the several groups called ''Apoda'' or ''Apodes''.

'''Apoda''' (ap′ọ-dä̤), ''n. pl.'' [NL., neut. pl. of ''apus'' (''apod-''), < Gr. ἄπους (ἀποδ-), footless: see ''apod''.] In ''zoöl.'', a name given to various groups of animals. {{smaller|(''a'') As used by Aristotle, the third division of ''Zoötoka'', or air-breathing animals which bring forth their young alive. It included the whales. This probably original use of the word still lingers in some systems. See (''b''). (''b'') Those placental mammals which have no feet, as distinguished from the ''Pedota'' (which see). (''c'') In ''ichth.'', same as ''Apodes''. (''d'') In Cuvier’s system of classification, the second order of echinoderms, contrasted with ''Pedicellata''. It is a heterogeneous group, consisting of the following genera: ''Molpadia'', ''Minyas'', ''Priapulus'', ''Lithodermis'', ''Siphunculus'', ''Bonellia'', ''Thalassema''; the first a holothurian, the second a cœlenterate, the rest gephyreans. (''e'') With Van der Hoeven, an order of echinoderms. See ''Gephyrea''. (''f'') In Claus’s arrangement, an order of holo-}}

